{ Original Scandinavian Defense course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qa5 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 c6 6. Bc4 Bf5 7. Bd2 e6 8. Qe2 Bb4 9. a3 Nbd7 10. O-O-O Bxc3 11. Bxc3 { Laboratory starts here. Qe2 prepares long castling and increases central pressure. ...Bb4 develops with a concrete relation to the c3 knight rather than immediately making another pawn move. Finish development and decide whether the queen should retreat before castling. If kings go to opposite wings, count attack tempi rather than following a fixed pawn storm. } 11... Qc7 { Candidate comparison. Places the queen on c7. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 12. Ne5 Nd5 13. g4 Nxe5 14. dxe5 Bg6 15. Bd2 h5 { Candidate comparison. Creates a wing or central pawn contact and changes king shelter. Calculate captures and the opponent's central counterplay before extending the advance. } 16. h3 hxg4 17. hxg4 Rxh1 18. Rxh1 O-O-O 19. Bg5 Re8 { Candidate comparison. Places the rook on the e-file. It can support central pawns or pressure the opposing center after exchanges. } 20. Kb1 Nb6 21. Ba2 *

