{ Original Scandinavian Defense course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 d5 2. e5 c5 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. Bb5 Bd7 5. Bxc6 Bxc6 6. d4 e6 7. Nc3 Ne7 8. Ne2 Nf5 9. a4 b5 10. axb5 Bxb5 11. O-O { Laboratory starts here. White declines the central exchange and gains space. ...c5 challenges the base that White may build with d4, while ...Nc6 adds central pressure. Develop the king side and decide whether ...e6 or ...Bg4 best fits White's setup. Attack the pawn chain rather than automatically bringing the queen out. } 11... cxd4 { Candidate comparison. The pawn captures a pawn on d4. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 12. Re1 Bb4 13. Bd2 Bxd2 14. Qxd2 Bxe2 15. Rxe2 Qb6 { Candidate comparison. Places the queen on b6. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 16. b3 Rc8 17. Ra4 O-O 18. g4 Ne7 19. Nxd4 Nc6 { Candidate comparison. Places the knight on c6, attacking knight on d4. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 20. Nf3 a5 21. Qe3 *

