{ Original Scandinavian Defense course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. c4 Qd8 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 e6 6. Nc3 c5 7. Be2 cxd4 8. Qxd4 Be7 9. Bf4 O-O 10. Qe3 Ng4 11. Qc1 { Laboratory starts here. The c-pawn move gains a queen tempo but leaves d4 as a possible long-term target. Black retreats safely and develops. Develop the bishops and seek ...c5 or ...Bb4+ when appropriate. Study an isolated d-pawn structure if central exchanges leave White with one. } 11... e5 { Candidate comparison. Places the pawn on e5, attacking bishop on f4. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 12. Nxe5 Nxe5 13. Bxe5 Nc6 14. Bg3 Nd4 15. O-O Be6 { Candidate comparison. Places the bishop on e6. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 16. Rd1 Nxe2+ 17. Nxe2 Qa5 18. Nf4 Bg5 19. h4 Bh6 { Candidate comparison. Places the bishop on h6, attacking knight on f4. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 20. Qc2 Bxf4 21. Bxf4 *

